# BoltVault extension — local build configuration.
#
# Copy to `.env` (gitignored) and fill in. WXT loads `.env` from this directory
# and bakes these into the bundle at build time, so a change here needs a
# rebuild, not just a reload.
#
#   cp apps/extension/.env.example apps/extension/.env
#   pnpm --filter @boltvault/extension build
#
# Anything here can also be given inline for a one-off build, which beats
# editing the file when you only want it once:
#
#   WXT_BOLTVAULT_API=https://electroswap.io pnpm --filter @boltvault/extension build

# Which API the wallet talks to: GraphQL, the wallet routes (prices, history,
# sync relay, crash reports) and nothing else — signed statics always come from
# static.electroswap.io. Defaults to https://electroswap.io when unset.
#
# Settings › About shows the configured origin and says when it is not the
# default, so a build pointed at a laptop cannot quietly look like a release.
WXT_BOLTVAULT_API=http://localhost:3005

# The wallet API key (master plan §9.1).
#
# Signed, not sent — with one exception. Every call to `services/api` carries
# `X-BoltVault-Auth`, an HMAC over the method, path and body, so what appears in
# devtools is bound to one call, expires in a minute and is refused on replay.
# The routing service below is the exception: it predates that scheme and does a
# constant-time compare of the key itself against its own list, so the quote
# request carries `X-BoltVault-Key`. That costs nothing that was not already
# true — extracting the key from this bundle has always worked, which is why
# every route is rate-limited regardless of it.
#
# A client identifier, not a secret — every install ships the same one — so it
# buys access, not trust; every bound that matters is applied by the API
# regardless of it. It is still not committed: the value differs between a
# local API and production, and a key in git is a key you cannot rotate.
#
# Without it the key-gated routes answer 401 and the engine falls back to
# calling GeckoTerminal directly (the documented fallback, §9.4). With it,
# prices come from our own proxy, which holds the upstream keys and means no
# third party sees a user's IP beside the tokens they hold.
#
# Must match one entry in the API's `BOLT_WALLET_API_KEY` (a CSV, so an
# environment can accept several keys at once — which is how you rotate).
# EXACTLY: a signature either verifies or it does not. A mismatch used to
# degrade to the GeckoTerminal fallback; it is now a 401 on every wallet route
# and on the GraphQL gate, which the extension has no other way through.
WXT_BOLTVAULT_KEY=

# Where the routing service lives (master plan §8.6).
#
# The wallet asks ElectroSwap's own router for a quote before falling back to
# the mini-router it runs against the chain, because the service walks the whole
# pool graph rather than sixteen fixed candidate paths. A failure of any kind —
# unreachable, slow, rate limited, no route, or a route the encoder cannot
# express — is silent and costs one bounded round trip; the on-chain quote still
# happens and the user is never told a service was involved.
#
# Leave empty for a release: ElectroSwap mounts the quoter at `/routing/quote`
# on the API origin above, and that is what the wallet derives. A quoter run
# locally (`services/quoter-api`, port 3007) serves the same handler at
# `/api/quote`, which the origin does not imply, so name it outright:
#
#   WXT_QUOTER_URL=http://localhost:3007/api/quote
#
# Its `BOLT_WALLET_API_KEY` must contain the key above, or every quote 401s and
# the wallet quotes on chain only.
WXT_QUOTER_URL=

# Turn on limit orders in this build.
WXT_BOLTVAULT_LIMIT_ORDERS=0
